Q: Chrome PWA no longer calculating dvh properly on initial load A: <p>I encountered this issue and ended up solving it by setting the height to <code>100svh</code> inside of an installed app.</p>
<pre class="lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>body {
  width: 100dvw;
  height: 100dvh;
  margin: 0;
}

/* Fix a Android Chrome bug where page is scrollable in an installed app after a reload */
@media (display-mode: standalone) {
  body {
    height: 100svh;
  }
}
</code></pre>

Q: Is it possible to hold an appendable list of comma separated values usable by preprocessor? A: <p>You can make a &quot;appendable&quot; macro by including a file with predefined slots with predefined types and incrementing a counter. I call this &quot;slots generic method&quot;. Below I will use &quot;Y_&quot; as an arbitrary example project namespace. Create a file &quot;y_slots.h&quot; with:</p>
<pre><code>// y_slots.h
#if Y_COUNTER == 1
#undef Y_COUNTER
#define Y_COUNTER 2
#define Y_SLOTS(X) \
   X(Y_TYPE_1, Y_FUNC_1)
#elif Y_COUNTER == 2
#undef Y_COUNTER
#define Y_COUNTER 3
#define Y_SLOTS(X) \
   X(Y_TYPE_1, Y_FUNC_1) \
   X(Y_TYPE_2, Y_FUNC_2)
#elif Y_COUNTER == 3
... etc ...
</code></pre>
<p>The <code>Y_SLOTS</code> is an X-macro funciton that takes a macro function that passes two arguments - the type and the function. Then we need a couple of defines:</p>
<pre><code>#define Y_CONCAT_(x, y)  x##y
#define Y_CONCAT(x, y) Y_CONCAT_(x, y)

// define initial value
#define Y_COUNTER 1

// Redefine TYPE,FUNC -&gt; Y_TYPE_[0-9], Y_FUNC_[0-9] to align with Y_SLOTS
#define Y_ADD_TYPE(TYPE, FUNC) \
   typedef TYPE Y_CONCAT(Y_TYPE_,Y_COUNTER); \
   TYPE Y_CONCAT(Y_TYPE_,Y_COUNTER); \
   static inline int Y_CONCAT(Y_FUNC_,Y_COUNTER)(TYPE* t) { return FUNC(t); }

// Call Y_SLOTS with something that will populate Generic expression.
// Note: leading ,
#define Y_SLOTS_CB_(type, func)  ,type: func
#define Y_GENERIC(x)  _Generic((x) Y_SLOTS(Y_SLOTS_CB))
</code></pre>
<p>Then it can be used like:</p>
<pre><code>int do_thing_with_float(int *t) { ... }
Y_ADD_TYPE(int, do_thing_with_int)
#include &lt;y_slots.h&gt;

int do_thing_with_float(float *t) { ... }
Y_ADD_TYPE(float, do_thing_with_float)
#include &lt;y_slots.h&gt;

int main() { 
   int (*f)(int *t) = Y_GENERIC(int);
}
</code></pre>
<p>I have experimented with this in my <a href="https://gitlab.com/Kamcuk/yio/-/blob/3a8a5a96aedfef1969ae67dd655f7a03c639fe3f/src/yio/yio/manip/slots.h#L14" rel="nofollow noreferrer">yio project</a> that worked <a href="https://gitlab.com/Kamcuk/yio/-/blob/3a8a5a96aedfef1969ae67dd655f7a03c639fe3f/examples/yio_example_custom_type_callback.c#L28" rel="nofollow noreferrer">like this</a>.</p>
<p>Note: there is no requirement for <code>Y_SLOTS</code> to be an X-macro, it could just straight expand into <code>#deifne Y_SLOTS  ,Y_TYPE_1:Y_FUNC_1</code>. However that X-macro makes it much easier later when you want to add types or do some logic.</p>
<p>Another note: creating macros with long fat single macro <code>#define INIT_TEMPLATE(type)</code> does not scale that much. It is ok for a few functions, but writing full modules in that style becomes unmaintainable. This is why the <a href="https://github.com/stclib/STC" rel="nofollow noreferrer">STC</a> switched to the &quot;include style&quot; of generic - i.e. <code>#define i_prefix PREFIX_ #define i_type &lt;type&gt; #include &lt;header_that_uses_i_type_and_i_name.h&gt;</code>.</p>
